-- What an input nobody supplied does where the evaluator needs its value.
--
-- Every input in the section below is unsupplied, so it is an unknown, and a
-- directive that needs its value stops with "I needed to know the value of …"
-- (`Stuck`), naming it. Until UNKNOWN-EVALUATION-SPEC §8 step 1, several
-- sites reported something else: a CONSIDER took a catch-all branch on the
-- unknown and printed that branch's answer as though it were the result; an
-- exhaustive CONSIDER, and every field read on an unknown record, said it had
-- no branch for the value; `3 EQUALS n`, and a literal pattern matched against
-- `n`, blamed the type; the string and JSON coercions blamed the type or
-- reported an internal error; the temporal iterators blamed the predicate;
-- and `#EVAL x` printed `x` as a value. A CONSIDER read by the regulative
-- action matcher, in an event's act or in a PROVIDED condition, answered on
-- the catch-all's guess too. The row numbers below are §4.12's.
--
-- `l4 run` EXITS 1 ON THIS FILE, BY DESIGN: a directive that cannot evaluate
-- is a crash (ruled by Meng 2026-08-01; see L4.Cli.Run.evalDirectiveFailsRun).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

DECLARE Kind IS ONE OF Retail, Wholesale
DECLARE Claim HAS kind IS A Kind, amount IS A NUMBER
DECLARE Person HAS age IS A NUMBER
DECLARE Pair HAS num IS A NUMBER, tag IS A STRING
DECLARE Triple HAS first IS A NUMBER, second IS A NUMBER, third IS A NUMBER
DECLARE Box HAS kind IS A Kind

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Seller HAS name IS A STRING
DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Deliver HAS item IS A Kind
    Pay     HAS amount IS A NUMBER, memo IS A STRING

alice MEANS Seller OF "Alice"

GIVETH A NUMBER
undecided MEANS REFUSE "not decided"

GIVETH A NUMBER
zero MEANS 0

GIVETH A NUMBER
fee MEANS REFUSE "the fee is not decided"

GIVEN it IS A Kind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is for sale` it MEANS
    CONSIDER it
    WHEN Wholesale THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN it IS A Kind
GIVETH A Kind
`same kind` it MEANS
    CONSIDER it
    WHEN Retail    THEN Retail
    WHEN Wholesale THEN Wholesale

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`must deliver retail` MEANS
    PARTY alice
    MUST   Deliver Retail
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`must deliver for sale` MEANS
    PARTY alice
    MUST   Deliver it PROVIDED `is for sale` it
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`must not deliver for sale` MEANS
    PARTY alice
    SHANT  Deliver it PROVIDED `is for sale` it
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`must pay rent` MEANS
    PARTY alice
    MUST   Pay (fee) "rent"
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH

DECIDE total IS 1 PLUS 2 PLUS 3

GIVEN z IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
double z MEANS z TIMES 2

§ `Unknown inputs`
    GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
          n IS A NUMBER
          m IS A MAYBE NUMBER
          d IS A Person
          k IS A Kind
          b IS A Box
          xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
          f IS A FUNCTION FROM NUMBER TO NUMBER
          s IS A STRING
          dt IS A DATE

GIVEN day IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`holds on` day MEANS x

-- CONSIDER on an unknown: stuck, naming it (rows 34 and 70).
#EVAL CONSIDER m WHEN NOTHING THEN 1, WHEN JUST y THEN 2
#EVAL CONSIDER m WHEN JUST y THEN 2, OTHERWISE 1
#EVAL CONSIDER x WHEN TRUE THEN 1, OTHERWISE 2
#EVAL CONSIDER xs WHEN EMPTY THEN 0, OTHERWISE 1
#EVAL CONSIDER xs WHEN h FOLLOWED BY t THEN h, OTHERWISE 0

-- A field read on an unknown record: stuck on the record at build step 1,
-- and on the field from build step 3, which names it `d's age` (row 35).
#EVAL d's age

-- An unknown inside a known value. The first branch fails whatever `k` is,
-- because `5` is not `0`, so the second is the answer (row 71) ...
#EVAL CONSIDER Claim k 5 WHEN Claim Retail 0 THEN "first", WHEN Claim kk a THEN "second"
-- ... but here the first branch matches if `k` is `Retail`, so it is stuck.
#EVAL CONSIDER Claim k 0 WHEN Claim Retail 0 THEN "first", WHEN Claim kk a THEN "second"
-- The same for an unknown met by a literal pattern: "x" is not "y" ...
#EVAL CONSIDER Pair n "x" WHEN Pair 0 "y" THEN "first", WHEN Pair a b THEN "second"
-- ... and here the first branch matches if `n` is `0`.
#EVAL CONSIDER Pair n "y" WHEN Pair 0 "y" THEN "first", WHEN Pair a b THEN "second"
-- A clash counts only if the match reaches it: here it would first read
-- `undecided`, which refuses, so the branch could refuse and this is stuck.
#EVAL CONSIDER Triple n undecided 5 WHEN Triple 0 7 0 THEN "first", WHEN Triple x y z THEN "second"
-- An expression pattern is evaluated before any later position is read, so a
-- later clash never stops it raising or refusing. A harmless one, then the
-- clash: "second" ...
#EVAL CONSIDER Pair n "x" WHEN Pair (EXACTLY zero) "y" THEN "first", WHEN Pair a b THEN "second"
-- ... and ones that raise or refuse, with EXACTLY and in brackets.
#EVAL CONSIDER Pair n "x" WHEN Pair (EXACTLY (1 DIVIDED BY 0)) "y" THEN "first", WHEN Pair a b THEN "second"
#EVAL CONSIDER Pair n "x" WHEN Pair (EXACTLY undecided) "y" THEN "first", WHEN Pair a b THEN "second"
#EVAL CONSIDER Pair n "x" WHEN Pair (1 DIVIDED BY 0) "y" THEN "first", WHEN Pair a b THEN "second"
-- The same in an event: the obligation names `fee`, which refuses, so this
-- refuses whatever `n` is.
#TRACE `must pay rent` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Pay n "deposit" AT 1

-- EQUALS names an unknown on either side (row 12), and so does a literal
-- pattern, which compares the literal with the value considered ...
#EVAL 3 EQUALS n
#EVAL n EQUALS 3
#EVAL "a" EQUALS s
#EVAL (Date 1 1 2024) EQUALS dt
#EVAL Person 3 EQUALS d
#EVAL CONSIDER n WHEN 0 THEN "zero", OTHERWISE "other"
-- ... while a function on the left is still an unsupported equality.
#EVAL double EQUALS f

-- The coercions (rows 83 and 84).
#EVAL n AS STRING
#EVAL TOSTRING n
#EVAL JSONENCODE n
#EVAL JSONENCODE (LIST 1, n)
#EVAL JSONENCODE (1 FOLLOWED BY xs)

-- A temporal iterator whose predicate returns an unknown.
#EVAL `EVER BETWEEN` (Date 5 1 2024) (Date 7 1 2024) `holds on`
#EVAL `ALWAYS BETWEEN` (Date 5 1 2024) (Date 7 1 2024) `holds on`
#EVAL `WHEN LAST` (Date 5 1 2024) `holds on`
#EVAL `WHEN NEXT` (Date 5 1 2024) `holds on`

-- A bare unknown as the result: stuck, as #ASSERT already reported it (row 52).
#EVAL x
#EVAL TRUE AND x
#EVAL TRUE IMPLIES x

-- A CONSIDER the action matcher reads on an unknown: in a PROVIDED, where the
-- catch-all used to make these FULFILLED and BREACHED, ...
#TRACE `must deliver for sale` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Deliver k AT 1
#TRACE `must not deliver for sale` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Deliver k AT 1
-- ... and in the act, through an exhaustive CONSIDER and through a field read.
#TRACE `must deliver retail` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Deliver (`same kind` k) AT 1
#TRACE `must deliver retail` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Deliver (b's kind) AT 1

-- An input is a value in every case, so it never refuses: failed (row 72).
#ASSERT REFUSED x

-- An unknown that is only carried along is printed as it is (row 60).
#EVAL LIST n, total
